AWS Region vs Availability Zone

🔍 What’s the Difference Between AWS Region and Availability Zone (AZ)?

🟦 AWS Region
A Region is a geographical location that contains at least two or more Availability Zones.
✅ Example: us-east-1 (N. Virginia), ap-south-1 (Mumbai)
 ✅ AWS has 30+ Regions across the globe
 ✅ You choose a Region closest to your users to reduce latency

🟨 Availability Zone (AZ)
An Availability Zone is a physically isolated data center (or a group of them) within a Region.
✅ Each Region has 2 or more AZs
 ✅ AZs are connected with high-speed private links
 ✅ AZs allow high availability & fault tolerance
🧠 Simple Analogy:
🗺️ Think of a Region as a city, and AZs as different buildings in that city.

 If one building (AZ) has a power outage, others are still available — your app stays up!
🚀 Why It Matters:
You deploy across AZs for high availability (like with RDS Multi-AZ or Auto Scaling groups)
AZs help ensure your apps stay online during failures
Always select the right Region for data residency & compliance
